"An earlier lesson in this unit described acupuncture points as fixed, mapped locations along the twelve meridians, each with its own name and traditional set of effects. Chinese medicine also recognizes a second, much less formal category: points with no fixed location at all, found simply by pressing around an area until a person reacts to a tender spot. These are called \"ashi points,\" a name that comes from the sound a person makes when the press finds the right place — roughly, \"ah, yes, that's it.\"": "5f93ddef214f",
"An earlier lesson in this unit described acupuncture points as fixed, mapped locations along the twelve meridians, each with its own name and traditional set of effects. Chinese medicine also recognizes a second, much less formal category: points with no fixed location at all, found simply by pressing around an area until a person reacts to a tender spot. These are called \"ashi points,\" a name that comes from the sound a person makes when the press finds the right place — roughly, \"ah, yes, that's it.\" An ashi point isn't marked on any traditional chart, doesn't have a name of its own, and can appear in a completely different spot from one person to the next, or even from one week to the next in the same person. What makes it worth treating isn't its location on a map — it's the local tenderness itself, read as a sign that qi and blood have become stuck in that specific spot, regardless of which meridian happens to run nearby. This category matters for understanding acupuncture as a living practice rather than a fixed lookup table. Alongside points chosen because a chart says they affect a particular organ, a practitioner is also always working with what the body is showing right now, in real time, at that specific visit — the fixed map and the moment-to-moment finding work together rather than one replacing the other.": "65fabdbae88d",
